{#-
  service_health.txt.j2

  Rendered TWICE from the same data by playbooks/reporting/service_health.yml:

    report_mask: true    -> the distribution copy, host names masked
    report_mask: false   -> the archive copy, mode 0600, full names

  ONE TEMPLATE, NOT TWO. Separate masked and unmasked templates drift, and the
  two drifts are not equally visible: a field added to the archive copy and
  forgotten in the masked one is a MISSING FIELD, which somebody notices; a
  field added to the masked one without its mask is a LEAK, which nobody does.
  Branching inside one template means every name renders through the same code
  path and the mask applies by construction.

  INPUT: host_rows, a list of dicts. The template never touches hostvars, so
  tests can drive it from fixtures with no hosts present.

      name        str   host identifier
      reachable   bool  false -> no checks at all, never zeroed counts
      verdict     str   PASS | FAIL | SKIP
      checks      list  of check / status / expected / actual / reason
      reason      str   present only when reachable is false
-#}
{%- macro hostname(value) -%}
{#- Names render through this macro or they do not render. mask_hostname
    RAISES on input it cannot parse rather than passing it through, so an
    unexpected value fails the render instead of landing unmasked in a report
    labelled masked. -#}
{%- if value is not defined or value in (none, '') -%}
-
{%- elif report_mask -%}
{{ value | mask_hostname }}
{%- else -%}
{{ value }}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
================================================================================
SERVICE AND CERTIFICATE HEALTH
Generated {{ report_generated }} UTC
{% if report_mask %}
MASKED COPY. Host names are partially redacted for distribution. The
unredacted copy is archived on the control node.
{% else %}
UNMASKED COPY. Contains full host names. Do not forward.
{% endif %}
================================================================================

SUMMARY
  Hosts in scope    {{ host_rows | length }}
  Collected         {{ host_rows | selectattr('reachable') | list | length }}
  Not collected     {{ host_rows | rejectattr('reachable') | list | length }}

  Checks PASS       {{ host_row_counts.PASS }}
  Checks FAIL       {{ host_row_counts.FAIL }}
  Checks SKIP       {{ host_row_counts.SKIP }}

{#- SKIP is printed on its own line with its meaning spelled out. A reader
    scanning three numbers will otherwise read the third as a minor variant of
    the first. It is not: an unevaluated check is not a healthy one, and the
    run fails on it by default. See docs/gotchas.md entry 16. -#}
  A SKIP is NOT a pass. It means the check could not be evaluated, and the
  thing it was supposed to check has an unknown state.

{% for row in host_rows | sort(attribute='name') %}
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
{{ hostname(row.name) }}  [{{ row.verdict }}]
{% if not row.reachable %}
  NOT COLLECTED -- {{ row.reason | default('no session established') }}
{#- No counts here on purpose. "0 failed" for a host nobody reached renders as
    a measurement and reads as the healthiest machine in the fleet. See
    docs/gotchas.md entry 3. -#}
  No checks were run on this host. Its service and certificate state is
  unknown, not healthy.
{% else %}
{% for check in row.checks %}
  {{ '%-6s' | format(check.status) }} {{ check.check }}
{%- if check.status != 'PASS' %}
           expected: {{ check.expected | default('') }}
           actual:   {{ check.actual | default('') }}
{% if check.reason | default('') %}           reason:   {{ check.reason | trim }}
{% endif %}
{%- endif %}
{% else %}
  No checks were configured for this host.
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
{#- The summary sections below are derived from host_rows, NOT from the flat
    host_rows_skipped / host_rows_failed lists the playbook builds.

    Those lists exist for the assert messages the playbook prints to the
    operator's own terminal, where full names are fine. Rendering them HERE was
    a leak: they are plain strings with the host name already baked in, so they
    bypassed the hostname() macro entirely and every name appeared unmasked in
    a file labelled masked -- while the per-host rows above masked correctly,
    which is exactly what made it look right.

    tests/test_health_report_masking.yml caught this. The fix is not to mask
    the strings, it is to delete the second data path: everything that renders
    a host name goes through host_rows and through the macro. -#}
{% if host_row_counts.SKIP | int > 0 %}

UNEVALUATED CHECKS
{% for row in host_rows | sort(attribute='name') %}
{% if not row.reachable %}
  {{ hostname(row.name) }} :: host -- {{ row.reason | default('no session established') }}
{% endif %}
{% for check in row.checks | selectattr('status', 'eq', 'SKIP') %}
  {{ hostname(row.name) }} :: {{ check.check }}
{% endfor %}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{% if host_row_counts.FAIL | int > 0 %}

FAILED CHECKS
{% for row in host_rows | sort(attribute='name') %}
{% for check in row.checks | selectattr('status', 'eq', 'FAIL') %}
  {{ hostname(row.name) }} :: {{ check.check }}
{% endfor %}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
